re  energy to extract it  if the water's mixed up in the soil it  might be a little easier  regardless knowing more about that water  how it gets there  the form in which it's present how much  of it exists  whether it moves around all of this is  very useful information for planning  possible resource utilization on the  moon  it's also important to understand how  stable the water is  and how easily disturbed or lost it  might be  as well as whether or not it might be  replenished over time  or if it's lost for good from the  location once we sample or use it  future missions like nasa's volatiles  investigating polar exploration rover or  viper  will be looking for water directly on  the surface and up to a meter below the  surface  to help make the first water resource  maps of the moon  you can see that this exciting discovery  leads to many new questions about water  on the moon  in our future science and exploration  activities and now we'll hear from  nassim rangwala sophia's project  scientist  thanks jacob as paul mentioned sophia is  an airborne observatory  by flying at high altitudes up to 45 000  feet sophia is able to get above 99.9  of the water vapor in earth's atmosphere  that is blocking the infrared light from  space  specifically for this discovery sophia  allowed us to detect water  on the sunlit surface of the moon  without contamination  from terrestrial water sofia carries an  instrument  that is able to pick up the specific  wavelength  unique to water molecules at 6.1 microns  as you heard from casey in fact  sophia is currently the only telescope  on or off this world that can provide  remote access to this unique chemical  fingerprint of water  at 6.1 microns normally  we study distant and much dimmer objects  like black holes star clusters and  galaxies  this was actually the first time sophia  observed the moon  and we did it as a test case  because the questions surrounding the  moon's water were so compelling  they decided to use the observatory in a  way not previously  imagined so in august of 2018  we decided to try a test observation on  the moon  this discovery was made while we were  flying over nevada  on our way back to our home base in  palmdale california  what was essentially a test far exceeded  our expectations  now that we know we can do this and  given the importance of  understanding the distribution of water  on the moon and the origin of water in  our solar system  we are planning more flights with sofia  to collect  more data we are very excited that these  follow-up observations  will look for water in more sunlit  locations to better  understand how water is created stored  and moves across the lunar surface over  time  these observations will add to the data  that nasa's next moon rover  the viper mission will collect to create  the first water resource map of the moon  back to you paul thanks nasim  nasa's space observatories allow us to  study the universe  in ways that are impossible from the  ground  and although sofia is not a space  observatory  it's an airborne observatory it does  allow us a view of the universe in the  far infrared  that is impossible from the ground  because the water vapor in the earth's  atmosphere  blocks this part of the electromagnetic  spectrum  today we are announcing an important  discovery by sofia  that the previously detected hydrogen on  the sunlit surface of the moon  is located in water molecules  this discovery raises new questions  about how water is  created on the surface of the moon and  how it can persist  in the harsh airless sunlit conditions  of the lunar surface knowing how much  water is on the moon  and how we might be able to use it in  the future  supports nasa's long-term human  exploration goals into deep space  nasa will continue to leverage all of  his assets  including sofia the world's premier  airborne observatory  to contribute to the success of the  artemis mission and nasa's human  exploration program  alicia thanks paul  media resources for this announcement is  available on nasa.gov  sophia and can also be found on our  press release  we will now start the question and  answer session sarah noble  headquarter lunar scientist will also be  joining us to cover your questions  we will limit everyone to one question  our operator should identify you  but if not please identify yourself your  media affiliation  and then direct your question to a  specific panelist if possible  for those dialing in push star 1 keys on  your phone to be placed into queue  to ask a question using twitter please  use the hashtag  asknasa and with that  let's begin let's start with jeff faust  from space news  to the moon whether they are orbiters or  some of the the landers beyond  obviously the viper rover might be used  to better characterize  the water signal that you saw with  sophia is there anything  currently on the books that would help  in that regard thanks  this is jacob i'm sorry we missed the  very beginning of your question  um could you maybe restate it please  sure let me let me try and summarize uh  are any of the upcoming missions whether  they are orbiters or  landers including payloads on clip  slanders  uh useful in helping to better  characterize the water signal you saw  with sophia  thanks yeah i can  i can give you a little background on on  some additional clips capabilities that  we're looking at  uh the the clips flights or commercial  lunar payload services  will leverage capabilities of industry  partners to quickly deliver scientific  instruments technology demonstrations to  the moon  as part of that nasa will be sending  dozens of science investigations and  technology experiments to the surface  that will help us find and potentially  extract later  resources on the moon so just an example  of some of these  that we're looking at moving forward one  would be the mass spectrometer observing  observing lunar operations or m solo  and solo will identify low molecular  weight volatiles  it can be installed to either measure  the lunar exosphere  or even things like spacecraft  outgassing and contamination because we  also want to understand what our  presence might be doing to the the water  and the  the precious volatiles there so data  gathered from m solo  will help determine the composition and  concentration  of these potentially accessible  resources another example would be a  neutron spectrometer system  nss nss will search for indications of  water ice near the lunar surface  by measuring how much hydrogen bearing  materials are at a landing site  and help determine the overall bulk  composition of the regolith around it  another example would be the near  infrared volatile  spectrometer or nervus nervus will  measure surface and subsurface hydration  carbon dioxide methane all resources  that could potentially be  mined from the moon and used by our  future explorers  they also help map surface temperature  changes at the landing site  and we mentioned uh we mentioned viper  earlier  we also have another activity that will  be going ahead of viper  nasa's awarded eclipse provider uh  intuitive machines attacks  in order to fly the polar resources ice  mining experiment or prime  one so prime one is a drill  combined with a mass spectrometer and  we'll be heading to the moon  in late 2022. so this is a precursor  uh drill and it will  fly with which will fly  it is a precursor to the trident drill  that's going to fly with m solo on viper  so this is going to give us an example  of how exactly to use the more  enabled viper mission when we get it  there so we do have a series of  activities and payloads that are already  set up to help us start measuring this  and understand more about the  the environment and the volatiles there  great thank you uh next question is  marcia dunn from ap  oh sorry yes marshalltown from ap yes hi  can you hear me hello  yes um yes this is for um the lead  author um  ms hunnabal uh cou